sec.p:
PTMs can alter the energy of intermolecular interactions to various degrees, especially when considering the possibility of multi-site modifications (40–42). A recent study suggests that multisite phosphorylation provides a tunable mechanism for controlling protein-protein interaction strength: only after a critical number of sites have been phosphorylated, the homotypic interaction is weakened enough for the proteins to leave the condensed phase (30). Here, we investigate how such variability in PTM-induced interaction modifications affects the steady-state properties of phase separation. We model this feature of a PTM-driven system by introducing the parameter
Δε=εN−εP=1−εP \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \Delta \varepsilon=\varepsilon_{N}-\varepsilon_{P}=1-\varepsilon_{P}$$\end{document}
which quantifies the change in intermolecular interaction energy upon enzymatic modification. We refer to this property as the modification strength. An increase in Δε corresponds to a stronger phosphorylation effect, which disfavors the interaction between phosphorylated proteins and the rest of the system.

The chemical reactions included in the model are the kinase-mediated phosphorylation and a spontaneous dephosphorylation (we implicitly consider the phosphatase to be diffuse in the system). These reactions are schematized below, with particles not explicitly included in the system written within round brackets
(1)
(ATP)+K+N⇌(ADP)+K+P \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} (A T P)+K+N \rightleftharpoons(A D P)+K+P \tag{1} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
((2)
P⇌N+(Phosphate) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} P \rightleftharpoons N+(\text { Phosphate }) \tag{(2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
We label the forward reaction rates of equation 1, 2, i.e. reading the reactions from left to right, respectively R1f,R2f\begin{document}$R_{1}^{f}, R_{2}^{f}$\end{document}, while the backward rates R1b,R2b\begin{document}$R_{1}^{b}, R_{2}^{b}$\end{document} correspond to reading reactions 1, 2 from right to left.

Overall conversion rates N → P (RN→P) and P → N (RP →N) are thus defined as
((3)
RN→P=R1f+R2b \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} R_{N \rightarrow P}=R_{1}^{f}+R_{2}^{b} \tag{(3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
((4)
RP→N=R2f+R1b \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} R_{P \rightarrow N}=R_{2}^{f}+R_{1}^{b} \tag{(4} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
In the dilute limit, reaction rates can be written as follows
(5)
R1f=k1ρNρK⟨e−βΔU⟩ \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} R_{1}^{f}=k_{1} \rho_{N} \rho_{K}\left\langle e^{-\beta \Delta U}\right\rangle \tag{5} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(6)
R1b=k1e−βΔμ1ρPρK \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} R_{1}^{b}=k_{1} e^{-\beta \Delta \mu_{1}} \rho_{P} \rho_{K} \tag{6} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(7)
R2f=k2ρP \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} R_{2}^{f}=k_{2} \rho_{P} \tag{7} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(8)
R2b=k2e−βΔμ2ρN⟨e−βΔU⟩ \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} R_{2}^{b}=k_{2} e^{-\beta \Delta \mu_{2}} \rho_{N}\left\langle e^{-\beta \Delta U}\right\rangle \tag{8} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where k1 and k2 are microscopic transition probabilities, β is the inverse of kBT, ρi is the concentration of species i and ⟨e−βΔU⟩ represents the ensemble average of the Boltzmann factor used in the LDB algorithm. Backwards rates are suppressed by a factor eβΔμi≃106\begin{document}$e^{\beta \Delta \mu_{i}} \simeq 10^{6}$\end{document} with respect to their corresponding forward rates, which takes into account the chemical driving of implicit particles involved in reaction i. In our reaction scheme, Δμ1 = μAT P − μADP and Δμ2=μH2O−μPhos\begin{document}$\Delta \mu_{2}=\mu_{H_{2} O}-\mu_{\text {Phos }}$\end{document}.
